1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:03,105
Good evening from the OROS closing desk.

2
00:00:03,105 --> 00:00:09,005
This morning the system named no pick, so tonight there is nothing to grade.

3
00:00:09,005 --> 00:00:13,740
No setup, no trade, no loss — that is the discipline working.

4
00:00:13,740 --> 00:00:18,676
Why we grade in public: a forecast that is never checked is just content.

5
00:00:18,676 --> 00:00:24,085
Every morning call gets an evening verdict, and both go in the permanent record.

6
00:00:24,050 --> 00:00:27,786
On the day, the book is down 537 dollars.

7
00:00:27,786 --> 00:00:30,793
Target (TGT) leads, +1.5 percent.

8
00:00:30,793 --> 00:00:31,157
U.S.

9
00:00:31,157 --> 00:00:34,437
Global Jets ETF (JETS) lags at -3.4.

10
00:00:34,437 --> 00:00:39,358
The checklist is clear: nothing requires action today.

11
00:00:39,358 --> 00:00:41,985
Today's lesson is noise versus signal.

12
00:00:41,985 --> 00:00:45,649
Daily moves are weather; the weekly trend is climate.

13
00:00:45,649 --> 00:00:48,691
The system never exits on a scary afternoon.

14
00:00:48,691 --> 00:00:54,221
It acts only on weekly closes, because that is where the real information lives.

15
00:00:54,172 --> 00:01:02,235
And here is the book itself — the actual holdings, identical to this morning's emailed report.

16
00:01:02,235 --> 00:01:11,842
12 positions, led by Target, ticker TGT, Charles Schwab Corporation (The), ticker SCHW, GE Aerospace, ticker GE.

17
00:01:11,842 --> 00:01:23,508
Every line carries its share count, its entry, and its exit — because a position without a defined exit is not a position, it is a hope.

18
00:01:23,475 --> 00:01:28,203
Now the part that makes this system different: the loop.

19
00:01:28,203 --> 00:01:30,567
Every morning OROS proposes.

20
00:01:30,567 --> 00:01:40,952
Every evening it grades its own call, journals the verdict, and adjusts its own risk before it is allowed to propose again.

21
00:01:40,952 --> 00:01:44,836
Inside the book today: 5 positions up, 7 down.

22
00:01:44,836 --> 00:01:47,116
Best, TGT at +1.55 percent.

23
00:01:47,116 --> 00:01:48,889
Worst, JETS at -3.38.

24
00:01:48,889 --> 00:01:53,786
Both go in the journal — wins and losses get the same ink.

25
00:01:53,786 --> 00:02:00,794
The honest scoreboard reads: expectancy -8.37% per trade over 176 graded positions.

26
00:02:00,794 --> 00:02:07,717
Because that record is soft, the system has throttled itself to 25% position size.

27
00:02:07,717 --> 00:02:12,783
Nobody told it to — full size must be earned back with wins.

28
00:02:12,783 --> 00:02:24,941
What tonight's read hands to tomorrow's open: 245 names close the day in Stage 2; position size stays cut to 25% until the record earns it back.

29
00:02:24,915 --> 00:02:27,180
Over to the sports desk.

30
00:02:27,180 --> 00:02:36,521
Day 26 of the race: three books, the same one hundred thousand simulated dollars, since July sixth.

31
00:02:36,521 --> 00:02:42,844
In first place, the live self-rebuilding book, in blue, at 104,223.

32
00:02:42,844 --> 00:02:48,411
Then the index fund, in gray, 1.3 thousand back at 102,895.

33
00:02:48,411 --> 00:02:55,677
Then the frozen first portfolio, the green line, 4.3 thousand back at 99,941.

34
00:02:55,677 --> 00:02:58,319
The race has no finish line.

35
00:02:58,319 --> 00:03:02,660
Same books, same dollars, back at it tomorrow.

36
00:03:02,660 --> 00:03:07,567
Every line starts the same day with the same dollar.

37
00:03:07,567 --> 00:03:09,454
Nothing is restated.

38
00:03:09,454 --> 00:03:18,362
On screen, three equity lines grown from the same starting dollar: the frozen first book, the live book, and the index fund.

39
00:03:18,324 --> 00:03:25,659
All figures are simulated paper trading, graded and journaled by the OROS agents with no human intervention.

40
00:03:25,659 --> 00:03:27,900
Nothing here is financial advice.

41
